Residential and commercial properties that have experienced water damage know that it can escalate into a major problem if not attended to immediately. Even a small water leak can cause serious damage to building materials if repair is not performed as soon as possible, so some home or business owners often choose to take the task on their own to remove the water and prevent further damage.
The problem, however, is that improper water extraction can cause more harm than good. Water damage comes in different classes and categories, which also need different approaches in order to be mitigated the proper way. Fortunately, there are restoration specialists, such as ServiceMaster of Savannah, who have the training and equipment to restore the property to pre-disaster condition. To guide home and business owners alike, ServiceMaster of Savannah discusses the common causes, classes, and categories of water damage in this article.
What Causes Water Damage?
Water damage can come from many sources. Here are the most common.
Broken or leaking pipes. Many factors can cause the bursting or leaking of pipes in homes. Signs of broken or leaking pipes include dripping water, water stains on walls and ceilings, cracked tiles, and a moldy smell. Moisture detection devices can help trace invisible leaks and other damages down the line and prevent further problems from occurring.
Plumbing problems. Plumbing is susceptible to blockages and clogs. A small crack can develop into water damage. Malfunctioning drains, signs of moisture, or cracking and deformation of structural materials may indicate faulty plumbing. Call a licensed plumber to repair the problem and a professional water damage restoration company to restore the affected area.
Clogged gutters and drainage systems. Working gutters and drainage systems direct water away from the home. If they become clogged, water can overflow and seepage will occur, causing damage to the foundation or roof leaks. Clean gutters and drainage systems regularly to prevent the accumulation of debris.
Backups or overflows. Sewer backups and water overflows are caused by clogs down the pipeline. Sewer backups can also be caused by brutal storms or tree roots blocking the pipes. If not acted upon right away, these overflows can destroy flooring and carpeting. Have your pipes cleaned regularly and turn off water supply lines in the event of an overflow.
Broken appliances. Malfunctioning or damaged appliances can cause water leaks, which in turn can warp wood or cause mold growth. Refrigerators, washing machines, and dishwashers can undergo wear and tear and can start leaking unless they are regularly maintained. Have appliances checked routinely and replace any item that has become too old or too damaged to use.
HVACs. HVAC ducts can wear out and be damaged, leading to water fissure. Regular HVAC maintenance allows professionals to spot minor issues early which decreases the probability of incurring bigger problems or even system breakdown.
Basement or attic concerns. Basements or attics can potentially house moisture, which in turn causes mold growth. Have them inspected regularly.
Weather or natural concerns. Natural calamities such as flash floods, thunderstorms, and other severe weather phenomena can cause substantial water damage to property. While these occurrences cannot be avoided, steps can be taken to keep the home in good condition.
Other Causes of Water Damage
- Badly fitted bathroom tiles;
- Broken or poor fitting shower screens and curtains;
- Shower trays – broken or badly fitted;
- Baths – poorly plumbed in or unattended;
- Poorly maintained sealant around the bath and shower;
- Poorly maintained or open chimneys;
- Flashing around the chimney – damaged or poorly installed;
- Roof tiles – missing or broken;
- Poorly installed or broken windows;
- Defrosted fridges and ice machines;
- Damp proof course – failed or missing;
- Insufficient or low lying air bricks;
- Damp proof course – bridged;
- DIY, i.e. nailing into pipes;
- Corroded ball valves in toilet cisterns;
- Insufficient threshold on external doors;
- Poorly maintained boilers; and
- Hot water cylinders – poorly maintained.
Categories of Water Damage
ServiceMaster of Savannah is here to discuss the three (3) types of water damage as designated by the IICRC (the Institute of Inspection, Cleaning, and Restoration Certification).
CATEGORY 1: Clean Water
Category 1 water is that which escapes from a clean and sanitary source, such as faucets, toilet tanks, and drinking fountains. However, Category 1 can quickly become Category 2 water damage if not dealt with right away.
CATEGORY 2: Grey Water
Grey water is water that is contaminated with chemical, physical, or biological elements or water that travels through another material, such as insulation or drywall, which may cause illnesses.
CATEGORY 3: Blackwater
Blackwater contains bacteria, viruses, and other pathogens and is grossly unsanitary, causing severe sickness and even death if ingested. Category 3 water can come from sewer backups, flooding from rivers or streams, toilet overflows, and stagnant liquid that has begun to support the growth of bacteria.
